WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.700
Speaker 1: Florida Republicans are now looking&nbsp;
to push the state's 15 week abortion ban to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:09.960
six week abortion ban. And a few of you wrote&nbsp;
to me and said, David, this bill doesn't make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:16.080
any sense. Women often don't know that they're&nbsp;
pregnant before the six week mark, but that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:22.080
exactly why it makes perfect sense to the people&nbsp;
who are trying to completely ban abortion. It is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:27.120
a feature of this proposal that many women don't&nbsp;
know. They're pregnant before the six month mark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:31.800
not a bug. And we are going to discuss that&nbsp;
in more detail. The New York Times New York&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:37.800
Times reports Florida Republicans propose a six&nbsp;
week abortion ban. The bills would tighten the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:45.180
current 15 week limit and offer narrow exceptions.&nbsp;
Governor Ron DeSantis said he welcomed, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:53.940
pro-life legislation. There is very little that is&nbsp;
pro-life about this bill. If a ban passes, right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.940 --> 00:01:00.060
David Chen and Patricia Mosey for The New York&nbsp;
Times. If a ban passes and is signed into law by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:08.100
DeSantis, Florida would prohibit abortion before&nbsp;
many women even realize they are pregnant. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:13.920
bills had been telegraphed for weeks. Republican&nbsp;
leaders who controlled Tallahassee sought to build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:20.460
on the anti-abortion momentum generated by the&nbsp;
US Supreme Court's overturning of Roe v Wade.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:27.180
DeSantis, expected to run for president, said he&nbsp;
supports additional restrictions, but the bills&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:33.540
offer a reminder of the political complexities&nbsp;
that he must navigate. Abortion restrictions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:38.820
appeal to Republicans, but 64% of Florida&nbsp;
residents believe abortion should be legal in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:43.680
most or all cases. Now, that's actually you know,&nbsp;
this is a bigger trend with abortion in general,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:51.120
which is that if you go back to the start of the&nbsp;
Roe v Wade era, you find that today rather than a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:58.980
month ago, rather than a year ago today, support&nbsp;
for abortion being legal in most cases is higher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:06.600
than it has been at any time during that Roe v&nbsp;
Wade era. And so although the anti-abortion stuff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:15.720
is useful for raising money from certain pieces of&nbsp;
the electorate, it's useful for playing red meat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:21.480
games and throwing red meat out to supporters.&nbsp;
In practice, when you talk to Americans, although&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:25.740
there are still many Americans, of course, tens&nbsp;
of millions who are just against any abortion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:31.560
at any time period, For the most part, Americans&nbsp;
believe abortion should be legal in most cases.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:34.800
And so there is a political complexity.&nbsp;
But let's talk about the six week thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:45.840
This is the critical aspect to this as a de facto&nbsp;
way to just ban abortion completely. Why? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:52.200
pregnant women are considered to be four weeks&nbsp;
pregnant by the time they miss their period.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:56.580
Often that's the first sign of pregnancy. Now,&nbsp;
another thing that's important to to remember&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:04.260
is how closely any particular woman would be&nbsp;
tracking. This does depend on whether she's trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:13.080
to be pregnant or not. And we'll sort of get to&nbsp;
that. But the first day of your last period is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:18.780
technically day one of the pregnancy. If you have&nbsp;
a 28 day cycle, which is sort of like the average,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:25.380
so you're already four weeks pregnant at the&nbsp;
point at which you suspect you might be missing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:31.140
your period. If you're not actively trying to&nbsp;
conceive, you may not realize you're pregnant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:38.520
within the two weeks that are left after the miss&nbsp;
period. And miss periods can mean nothing. Right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:45.480
There could be a plus or minus seven days or&nbsp;
something like that. So now you're at five weeks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:52.020
but the limit is six weeks. I know people who did&nbsp;
IVF, in vitro fertilization, they were tracking.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:58.080
They were monitoring everything. They didn't know&nbsp;
they were pregnant until what would have been five&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:05.280
weeks into the pregnancy. Most Obies won't even&nbsp;
see a patient for a new pregnancy until the eight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:09.900
week mark unless you're having fertility issues&nbsp;
and they say, Come in, we're going to do an h, c,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:16.020
g. I believe it is blood test to figure out what's&nbsp;
going on and check the level. So by this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:20.940
you were you would no longer legally be able to&nbsp;
get an abortion in the state of Florida. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:27.180
the point. And so normal people would say, oh,&nbsp;
that makes it really difficult to get an abortion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:32.100
at all. That doesn't make sense. The people who&nbsp;
want the bill are saying, oh, that will make it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:37.860
really difficult to get an abortion at all. That's&nbsp;
exactly what we want without outright banning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:43.500
abortion. So this is something that Florida&nbsp;
Republicans are pushing. We're going to continue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:49.620
seeing this in other states. Some states have&nbsp;
really old laws that were that are still in place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:57.540
but that weren't relevant because of Roe v Wade&nbsp;
existence with the removal of Roe v Wade. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.540 --> 00:05:04.080
those laws are once again being revived&nbsp;
and evaluated from a state Supreme Court&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:09.060
standpoint in some states. So this is all going to&nbsp;
get worse and worse and worse. Now, quite frankly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:15.840
I don't think Republicans at the federal level&nbsp;
are really going to be running on this because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:22.920
they know the numbers. And in fact, I may be&nbsp;
wrong, but in this nascent stage of his candidacy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:29.040
I don't think I've heard Trump talk about abortion&nbsp;
almost ever. And much of the focus when it comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:33.240
to the social and cultural issues has been on&nbsp;
transgender issues. And we've been talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:39.480
that and covering it. So we'll see where this&nbsp;
goes in Florida. We'll zoom out. And in a year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:45.120
we will see where the abortion landscape is.&nbsp;
But it is absolutely scary and horrifying stuff.

